																		Re: there IS a site-05, though. and a site-005. you need to be more specific.
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605795275" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1560195286 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Jun 2019 19:34</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4276933">
									

<p>So, I said that at some point I'd make a post describing what this skip is about and what's going on, here. Insert vague &quot;author head-canon is irrelevant, your interpretation is just as valid as mine&quot; prologue here:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">EXPLANATION:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This skip is about genocide denial.</p>
<p>This idea started way back when I was doing searches on the site and noticed there wasn't a &quot;Site-5&quot; anywhere. This immediately prompted me to try and write a skip that explained why &#8212; the result was an article I didn't feel satisfied with (for various reasons). I sat on the premise for a while.</p>
<p>Eventually, I had a new idea. I wanted to invoke the <a href="/scp-3790">Department of Abnormalities</a> without ever referencing them by name. The DoA (for those unfamiliar) is like the Foundation's dark, absent doppelganger &#8212; a specter of a past that never happened. Something another user (Scantron, aka <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=617958&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1560397310" alt="Communism will win" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=617958)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;">Communism will win</a></span>) once said about them always stuck with me: the DoA can be a metaphor for how we bury our horrible past so deeply that upon 'rediscovering' it, it looks like it belongs to someone else. This is what I realized I wanted to evoke.</p>
<p>With each revision, the Foundation rediscovers the site &#8212; and immediately ceases to investigate it. With each incident, they bury it a little deeper (plugging the stairs with concrete, paneling the doors and windows, deleting the files) &#8212; but it keeps coming back. People keep <em>remembering</em>.</p>
<p>I won't tell you what the blacked out text is, but I'll mention some words you might find if you peeked: &quot;congealed&quot;, &quot;mass&quot;, &quot;irradiated&quot;. There's a reason the containment procedures suddenly start talking about sterilizing &quot;personnel&quot; instead of documents (to evoke mass sterilization campaigns). There's a reason this takes place in Japan (on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hashima_Island">Hashima Island</a>, specifically). There's even a reason for the air sirens (connecting it to WW2).</p>
<p>Something so unspeakably horrible happened here that the Foundation has disassociated themselves from it in every way imaginable. This past is so toxic, so destructive, so corrosive that every time they rediscover it, it literally <em>rots</em> their brains until they bury it even deeper&#8230; only for it to seep through the cracks, and for them to rediscover it again.</p>
<p>SCP-4182 is about the idea of being haunted by a horrible curse, a ghost that you keep insisting isn't real &#8212; only to discover that the ghost is your very own.</p>
<p><span style="font-size:80%;">*spooky noises*</span></p>
</div>
</div>
