			<div class="content" id="post-content-4282652">
									

<p>I was initially off-put by certain aspects of the article and prepared to downvote, because this particular take on it didn't come to mind. I did see it as the Foundation trying to hide it, and previous iterations obviously being less and less redacted, but I thought that was a matter of them not wanting others to discover it (which was a bit obvious and lame if so). But I figured I should check the comments first to see if there was something I just wasn't seeing about it.</p>
<p>I didn't think of the notion that the Foundation was trying to bury it deeper because THEY didn't want to know about it, that they were hiding it from THEMSELVES. That thought legitimately takes this from being mediocre to being spectacular.</p>
<p>Enthusiastic upvote, though I wish there was some way to make that conclusion a bit easier to arrive at. The ghosts of your own past coming back to haunt you directly is a much more compelling story than just trying to hide your secrets from others. Something that refuses to allow itself to be completely forgotten. I love it.</p>

