			<div class="content" id="post-content-4276236">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>One of the things I wanted to achieve with the SCP was the notion of leading the reader deeper and deeper into perceiving Site-5 as a &quot;real&quot; place; this is why each iteration has the image of the island getting closer and closer (with the video being the climax &#8212; we're now <em>on</em> the island, physically exploring it). That being said, I remember thinking even as I was editing the video, 2 minutes is a bit much. The important aspect is the descent down the stairs &#8212; everything else is atmospheric fluff.</p>
<p>When I edit this thing later tonight/tomorrow (to deal with the sound issue), I'll probably try to see if I can cut the first half. I kept it because the scream you're hearing is actually something that genuinely happened (animals living in the building). I was so charmed by capturing something so organically spooky on camera that it felt weird cutting it.</p>
<p>The emoticon was to create tonal dissonance re: jdavis. I wanted the reader to be left confused about who he was, what he was doing, what his motivations are &#8212; or why he's been leading them along like this. To me, there's something very, <em>very</em> sinister about ending on such an innocuous smiley face.</p>
<p>Re: the repetitive nature, I get that; it's one of the reasons each iteration is so short (the longest clocks in somewhere under 500-600 words). I didn't want to use blue text, though, because I feel blue text calls attention to the fiction of the document itself &#8212; and I wanted to get the reader as deeply into a realistic space as possible.</p>
<p><strong>ETA:</strong> One thing I could change is reduce the size of the errors, which are actually just fluff and exist only to move the story along. I'd want to find something similarly 'real' looking, aesthetically, to replace them with though.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I appreciate the feedback!</p>

